\description{
\code{stat_summary_time()} is a temporal variation of \code{\link[ggplot2:stat_summary]{ggplot2::stat_summary()}}.
Rather than summarising the values at each unique time point, it summarises
the values within each instance of a time \code{granule} (e.g. each month).

A summary of a granule that is only partly observed is often misleading, such
as the total of a month which has only just started. The times within each
granule are checked with \code{\link[mixtime:time_is_complete_at]{mixtime::time_is_complete_at()}}, and implicit
missing values are filled with \code{NA} before the summary is computed. With
\code{na.rm = FALSE} (the default), functions like \code{\link[=mean]{mean()}} and \code{\link[=sum]{sum()}} then give
\code{NA} for incomplete granules, exactly as they do for explicit missing values.
}
\section{Aesthetics}{


\code{stat_summary_time()} requires the \code{x} and \code{y} aesthetics, one of which is
time. Other aesthetics are those of the \code{geom}, and are retained if they are
constant within each granule.
}

\section{Missing values}{


Within each group, every granule instance between its first and last
observations is summarised. A granule without observations is summarised from
a single \code{NA}, and so breaks a line rather than being drawn through.

Values outside the scale's limits are also missing. Use the limits of
\code{\link[ggplot2:coord_cartesian]{ggplot2::coord_cartesian()}} to zoom without affecting the summaries.

Some summary functions always ignore missing values, including
\code{\link[ggplot2:mean_se]{ggplot2::mean_se()}}. Use the computed variable
\code{complete} to identify incomplete granules in this case.
}

\section{Computed variables}{


These are available to \code{\link[ggplot2:after_stat]{ggplot2::after_stat()}}, alongside the results of the
summary functions:

\describe{
\item{\code{n}}{The number of non-missing values in the granule.}
\item{\code{complete}}{Whether the granule has no implicit or explicit missing
values.}
\item{\code{start}, \code{end}}{The boundaries of the granule, in data units.}
\item{\code{width}}{The \code{width} proportion of the granule, in data units.}
}
}

\examples{
library(ggplot2)

daily <- data.frame(
  date = mixtime::date(as.Date("2024-01-01") + 0:99),
  value = cumsum(rnorm(100))
)

# Monthly totals, where the last month is incomplete and so is missing
ggplot(daily, aes(date, value)) +
  stat_summary_time(granule = month(1L), fun = sum)

# Monthly means are on the same scale as the data
ggplot(daily, aes(date, value)) +
  geom_line(alpha = 0.3) +
  stat_summary_time(granule = month(1L), fun = mean, geom = "point")

# Summarise the available values, and highlight incomplete months
ggplot(daily, aes(date, value)) +
  stat_summary_time(
    aes(fill = after_stat(complete)),
    granule = month(1L), fun = mean, geom = "col", na.rm = TRUE
  )

# Hourly observations stored at a 1 second chronon
hourly <- data.frame(
  time = mixtime::datetime(
    as.POSIXct("2024-01-01", tz = "UTC") + 0:95 * 3600
  ),
  value = rnorm(96)
)
ggplot(hourly, aes(time, value)) +
  stat_summary_time(
    granule = day(1L), chronon = hour(1L), geom = "pointrange",
    fun = median, fun.min = min, fun.max = max
  )

}
